abstract: "Split likelihood-ratio (universal inference) confidence sets are valid at a fixed sample size. Pace, Salvan and Sartori (2023) conjectured that, recomputed after every new pair of observations with the same threshold, they keep asymptotic persistence 1 - epsilon. This package proves the conjecture false: asymptotic persistence is 0 in every model (zero-one law); the log split likelihood ratio has limsup/log log n = (sqrt5-1)/2 in regular models; split fractions and cross-fitting give c(r) and 1/K; a threshold repair is sharp on the iterated-logarithm scale and a checkpoint repair works in any model; an exact Ornstein-Uhlenbeck representation quantifies finite-horizon severity. Unrefereed candidate; internal AI-assisted review only. Text, data and diagrams CC0-1.0; code MIT."
